   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to notifying certain tenants of the senior citizen rent increase exemption program and the disability rent increase exemption program",
    "basePrintNo": "S5856",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5856-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §467-b, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5856\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the real property tax law, in\nrelation to notifying certain tenants of the senior citizen rent\nincrease exemption program and the disability rent increase exemption\nprogram\n\n PURPOSE :  To ensure that eligible tenants who qualify for the Senior\nCitizen Rent Increase Exemption (SCRIE) and the Disability Rent\nIncrease Exemption (DRIE) programs are made aware of such benefits\nwith their annual lease.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of this bill amends subdivision 3 of section 467-b of the\nReal Property Tax Law by adding a new paragraph i which provides that\nthe entity that administers the Senior Citizen Rent Increase Exemption\nProgram (SCRIE) and the Disability Rent Increase Exemption Program\n(DRIE) has to develop informational material describing eligibility\nfor and benefits of both these programs, and how more information can\nbe obtained for these programs. This information must be provided to\nlandlords of housing accommodations subject to the local emergency\nhousing rent control act, the emergency tenant protection act of 1974,\nthe emergency housing rent control law and the rent stabilization law\nof 1969.\n\nThis paragraph further provides that any landlord subject to these\nspecific housing accommodating provisions must at least once annually,\ninclude with a new lease and all renewal leases delivered to any\noccupant of those accommodations, provide the informational material\nthat describes eligibility for and the benefits of the SCRIE program\nand the DRIE program.\n\nSection two of this bill provides that this act shall take effect on\nthe thirtieth day after it shall have become a law, provided, however,\nthat the amendments to subdivision 3 of section 467-b of the Real\nProperty Tax Law made by section one of this act shall not affect the\nexpiration of such section and shall be deemed to expire therewith.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :  The Senior Citizen Rent Increase Exemption (SCRIE)\nand Disability Rent Increase Exemption (DRIE) Programs are wildly\nsuccessful programs put in place to protect one of the most vulnerable\ngroups of tenants, the elderly and disabled. This program has kept\ncountless seniors in their homes as rent continues to increase\nexponentially across the city. Yet, too often seniors and people with\ndisabilities are unaware of their options to apply for these programs.\n\nThis bill would require landlords to notify their rent-regulated\ntenants with their annual lease of the existence of the SCRIE and DRIE\nprograms and to provide information on how to access information about\nthe programs.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY : New Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS : None to the State\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :  This act shall take effect on the thirtieth day\nafter it shall have become a law, provided, however, that the\namendments to subdivision 3 of section 467-b of the real property tax\nlaw made by section one of this act shall not affect the expiration of\nsuch subdivision and shall be deemed to expire therewith.\n",
